⟦200⟧Since these equations must be true identically, the coefficients of <math>P^s_i</math> in each of them must vanish. Therefore
{{block center|<math>\alpha^s_i\{\sigma-i(i+1)\}+2\sigma^2\left\{\beta^s_{i+1}\frac{i+s+1}{2i+3}+\beta^s_{i-1}\frac{i-s}{2i-1}\right\}=0,</math>}}
{{block center|<math>-\beta^s_i\{\sigma+i(i+1)\}+\frac{4ma}{\gamma\sigma^2}C^s_i=0,</math>}}
{{block center|<math>b_i(C^s_i-E^s_i)+\alpha^s_{i-1}\frac{(i-s)[\sigma+(i-1)(i-2)]}{2(2i-1)}</math> (45)}}
{{block center|<math>+\alpha^s_{i+1}\frac{(i+s+1)[\sigma+(i+2)(i+3)]}{2(2i+3)}-\beta^s_is^2=0.</math>}}

⟦600⟧If we eliminate the <math>\alpha</math>'s and <math>\beta</math>'s from the third equation (45), by means of the first two, we find
{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|'''''Solution for Tesseral Oscillations.'''''}}
{{block center|<math>\xi^s_{i-2}C^s_{i-2}-L^s_iC^s_i+\eta^s_{i+2}C^s_{i+2}=\frac{\gamma b_i}{4ma}E^s_i,</math> (46)}}
where
{{block center|<math>L^s_i=\frac{s^2}{\sigma^2[\sigma+i(i+1)]}+\frac{(i^2-s^2)[\sigma+(i-1)(i-2)]}{(4i^2-1)[\sigma-(i-1)i][\sigma+i(i+1)]}</math>}}
{{block center|<math>+\frac{[(i+1)^2-s^2][\sigma+(i+2)(i+3)]}{[4(i+1)^2-1][\sigma-(i+1)(i+2)][\sigma+i(i+1)]}-\frac{\gamma b_i}{4ma}</math>}}
{{block center|<math>\xi^s_{i-2}=\frac{-(i-s)(i-s-1)}{(2i-1)(2i-3)[\sigma-(i-1)i]},</math>}}
{{block center|<math>\eta^s_{i+2}=\frac{-(i+s+1)(i+s+2)}{(2i+3)(2i+5)[\sigma-(i+1)(i+2)]}.</math>}}

⟦1100⟧In the case of the luni-solar semi-diurnal tide (called <math>\text{K}_2</math> in the notation of harmonic analysis) we have <math>i=2</math>, <math>s=2</math>, <math>\sigma=2</math>. Hence it would appear that these formulae for <math>L^s_i</math> and <math>\xi^s_{i-2}</math> fail by becoming indeterminate, but <math>i</math> and <math>s</math> are rigorously integers, whereas <math>\sigma</math> depends on the “speed” of the tide; accordingly in the case referred to we must regard terms involving <math>(i-s)</math> as vanishing in the limit when <math>\sigma</math> approaches to equality with <math>i\ (i-1)</math>. For this particular case then we find
{{block center|<math>L^2_2=\frac{3}{35}-\frac{\gamma b_2}{4ma}</math> and <math>\xi^s_0=0.</math>}}
The equation (46) for the successive <math>C</math>'s is available for all values of <math>i</math> provided that <math>C_{-1}</math>, <math>E_{-1}</math>, <math>C_0</math>, <math>E_0</math> are regarded as being zero.

⟦1500⟧As in the case of the zonal oscillations, the equations with odd suffixes separate themselves from those with even suffixes, so that the two series may be treated independently of one another. Indeed, as we shall see immediately, the series with odd suffixes are satisfied by putting all the <math>C</math>'s with odd suffixes zero for the case of such oscillations as may be generated by the attractions of the moon or sun.

⟦1700⟧For the semi-diurnal tides <math>i=2</math>, <math>s=2</math>, and <math>f</math> is approximately equal to unity. Hence the equilibrium tide is such that all the <math>E^s_i</math>, excepting <math>E^2_2</math>, are zero.

{{smaller block|⟦1800⟧For the diurnal tides <math>i=2</math>, <math>s=1</math>, and <math>f</math> is approximately equal to <math>\frac{1}{2}</math>. Hence all the <math>E^s_i</math>, excepting <math>E^1_2</math>, are zero. Since in neither case is there any <math>E</math> with an odd suffix, we need only consider those with even suffixes.}}

⟦2000⟧In both cases the first equation among the <math>C</math>'s is
{{block center|<math>-L^s_2C^s_2+\eta^s_4C^s_4=\frac{\gamma b_2}{4ma}E^s_2(s=2\ \text{or}\ 1).</math>}}
It follows that if we write
{{block center|<math>\xi^s_0C^s_0=-\frac{\gamma b_2}{4ma}E^s_2(s=2\ \text{or}\ 1),</math>}}
the equation of condition amongst the <math>C</math>'s would be of general applicability for all even values of <math>i</math> from 2 upwards.

⟦2300⟧The symbols <math>\xi^s_0</math>, <math>\eta^s_2</math> do not occur in any of the equations, and therefore we may arbitrarily define them as denoting unity, although the general formulae for <math>\xi</math> and <math>\eta</math> would give them other values. Accordingly we shall take
{{block center|<math>\xi^s_0C^s_0=C^s_0=-\frac{\gamma b_2}{4ma}E^s_2(s=2\ \text{or}\ 1).</math>}}
With this definition the equation
{{block center|<math>\xi^s_{i-2}C^s_{i-2}-L^s_iC^s_i+\eta^s_{i+2}C^s_{i+2}=0(s=2\ \text{or}\ 1)</math>}}
is applicable for <math>i=2</math>, 4, 6, &c.

⟦2600⟧It may be proved as in the case of the tides of long period that we may regard <math>C^s_i/C^s_{i+2}</math> as tending to zero. Then our equation may be written in the form
{{block center|<math>\xi^s_{i-2}\frac{C^s_{i-2}}{C^s_i}=L^s_i-\frac{\xi^s_i\eta^s_{i+2}}{\xi^s_iC^s_i/C^s_{i+2}},</math>}}
and by successive applications the right-hand side may be expressed in the form of a continued fraction. Let us write
{{block center|<math>H^s_i=\frac{\xi^s_{i-2}\eta^s_i}{L^s_i-}\,\frac{\xi^s_i\eta^s_{i+2}}{L^s_{i+2}-}\,\frac{\xi^s_{i+2}\eta^s_{i+4}}{L^s_{i+4}-}\dots</math>}}
Hence our equation may be written
{{block center|<math>\xi^s_{i-2}\frac{C^s_{i-2}}{C^s_i}=\frac{\xi^s_{i-2}\eta^s_i}{H^s_i}</math>}}

